  "description": "Chemical element with atomic number 1 (H)",
  "extract_html": "<p><b><a href=\"/school1/ptable/element/H\" class=\"wiki-link\" title=\"Hydrogen (H)\">Hydrogen</a></b> is a chemical element; it has the symbol<span class=\"nowrap\">\u00a0</span><b>H</b> and atomic number<span class=\"nowrap\">\u00a0</span>1. It is the lightest and most abundant chemical element in the universe, constituting about 75% of all normal matter. Under standard conditions, <a href=\"/school1/ptable/element/H\" class=\"wiki-link\" title=\"Hydrogen (H)\">hydrogen</a> is a gas of diatomic molecules with the formula<span class=\"nowrap\">\u00a0</span><span class=\"chemf nowrap\">H<sub class=\"template-chem2-sub\">2</sub></span>, called <b>dihydrogen</b>, or sometimes <b><a href=\"/school1/ptable/element/H\" class=\"wiki-link\" title=\"Hydrogen (H)\">hydrogen</a> gas</b>, <b>molecular hydrogen</b>, or simply hydrogen. Dihydrogen is colorless, odorless, non-toxic, and highly combustible. Stars, including the Sun, mainly consist of hydrogen in a plasma state, while on Earth, hydrogen is found as the gas<span class=\"nowrap\">\u00a0</span><span class=\"chemf nowrap\">H<sub class=\"template-chem2-sub\">2</sub></span> (dihydrogen) and in molecules, such as in water and organic compounds. The most common isotope of hydrogen, <sup>1</sup>H, consists of one proton, one electron, and no neutrons.</p>",
  "local_image": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d9/Hydrogenglow.jpg",
  "atomic_mass": 1.008,
